            AppRoutes = """["/sales-channels"]""",
            Tags = """["sales-channels","retail","marketplace"]""",
            ContentJson = """{"body":"## Sales Channels\n\nA sales channel answers three questions about every order: where did it come from, which account carries the money owed, and who is responsible for the sales tax. Every Forge install starts with one channel, **Direct**, for ordinary account business. You only need more when you sell somewhere else — your own web store, a marketplace like Amazon or Etsy, a trade-show table, or walk-in and phone sales.\n\nFind it under **Sales Channels** in the menu. Channels show as cards, split into two groups: **Account business** and **Retail and marketplaces**. Retired channels are hidden until you click **Show retired**.\n\n### Account business vs. retail\n\nAn **account** order is for a customer who has an account with you. Credit terms, quotes, price lists, and purchase orders all apply, and the customer owes you the money.\n\nA **retail** order is for a consumer. They paid at checkout, so there is no credit check, no quote, and no customer PO. The person who bought is recorded as a **retail buyer**, not a customer. The money owed lands on the channel's **house account** — one real customer record you set up to stand for that channel (for example, \"Amazon Marketplace\" or \"Web Store\"). That is what keeps aging, statements and accounting in step: the house account is the one that owes you, the buyer is the one who receives the goods.\n\n**Retail buyers are not customers.** They never appear in the Customers list, they have no credit or price lists, and there can be thousands of them. Keep them on the Retail Buyers page where they belong; do not create customer records for them.\n\n### Setting up a channel\n\nClick **New Channel**. Give it a **Name** and a short **Code** (the code can never change afterward, because order numbers and integrations key on it). Pick the **Channel type**: Account business, Direct retail, or Marketplace. The type also cannot change once saved — switching a live channel would change what its existing orders meant.\n\nFor a retail or marketplace channel you must choose **Bills to (house account)**. You can also attach a **Store connection** if orders should be pulled in from a storefront; leave it empty for a manual channel where orders are keyed in by hand.\n\n**Sales tax collected by** matters most on marketplaces. Many marketplaces collect and remit sales tax themselves under marketplace-facilitator law. When that is the case, choose the marketplace option — the tax still shows on the order because the buyer paid it, but it is never your liability and the card shows a **Marketplace collects tax** chip so no one reports it twice.\n\nAn **Order number prefix** starts a separate numbering series for that channel, so web orders can read WEB-1001 while account orders keep their usual numbers.\n\n### The default channel\n\nOne channel is always the **Default**. Any order created without picking a channel belongs to it. Use **Make default** to change it. A retail channel can never be the default, because orders created without a channel are account business. The default channel cannot be deleted; channels with orders or buyers against them cannot be deleted either — retire them instead.\n\n### Taking a retail order by hand\n\nWhen a retail channel exists, the **Take retail order** button appears on the Sales Channels page. Pick the channel, enter the buyer's name and contact details, the ship-to address, and the items with the price actually charged (retail prices are not looked up from a price list). Tick **Agreed to marketing contact** only if the buyer actually said yes — a sale on its own is not consent. Shipping charged and a tax rate go at the bottom, and the order total updates as you type.\n\n### Store connections\n\n**Sales Channels > Store Connections** holds the credentials for each storefront or marketplace account. A connection is credentials only; it does nothing until a channel is attached to it. A shop can hold several — two Shopify stores, or Shopify plus another platform — each feeding its own channel. Today Shopify and WooCommerce can be connected; other platforms are listed but not yet available, and the card says so.\n\nWhen you edit a connection the credentials field starts blank, and blank means **keep what is stored** — the stored secret is never shown back. Use **Test connection** to confirm the credentials work. **Import orders automatically** pulls new orders in on a schedule; **Push inventory levels back** keeps the storefront's stock counts in line with Forge.\n\n### Listings\n\n**Sales Channels > Channel Listings** lists every product (SKU) the storefront sells and the Forge part it ships from. Use **Sync from channel** to pull the storefront's catalog in, then click any row to map it to a part. A listing marked **Unmapped** still imports orders — its lines just arrive without a part until you map it, and mapping fills those earlier lines in. Turn on **Unmapped only** to see just the ones waiting for you. Clearing a mapping affects future orders only; lines that already shipped keep their part.\n\n### Settlements\n\nMarketplaces pay you in batches on their own cycle, net of referral fees, fulfilment fees, postage and refunds, so a deposit never matches a single invoice. **Sales Channels > Channel Settlements** imports those payout batches and ties each one back to the orders and fees inside it. Pick a marketplace and click **Import payouts**. A batch shows **Ties out** when the lines add up to what the marketplace said it paid, and **Needs review** when they do not. Open a batch to see the reported amount, the lines total, the variance, and any lines naming an order that was never imported. When a variance is genuine (a withheld reserve, a fee restated outside the batch), record the reason with **Accept variance** and the batch stops showing as an exception.\n\n### Buyer privacy\n\n**Sales Channels > Retail Buyers** lists everyone who bought through a retail or marketplace channel, with their order count, lifetime value and last order. Marketplace terms require you to remove personal details on request. The **Scrub personal details** action removes the buyer's name, email, phone and shipping addresses permanently while keeping their orders, counts and totals, and the row is marked **Scrubbed**.","sections":[]}"""
        });

